# CRC-16 Data Integrity Engine with Fault-Injection

Data gets corrupted over wires, radio links, and storage mediums all the time. A **flipped bit doesn't change the message length**, meaning a receiver has zero clue the data is garbage unless it explicitly checks it.  

This project is a **complete, hardware-level Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC) communication link**. Instead of just doing the math on paper, this implements a **full sender**, a **receiver**, and a **deliberate fault injector** to intentionally wreck the data in transit and prove that the error detection actually works.  

## The Hardware Blocks

*   **`crc16_core.vhd` (The Calculator):** The brains of the operation. It processes **one byte per clock cycle** using the **CRC-16/CCITT-FALSE** standard. It doesn't know anything about frames or messages; it strictly crunches the running checksum.  

*   **`crc16_tx.vhd` (The Sender):** A Moore state machine. It streams the message **"HELLO"** one byte per clock cycle, calculates the CRC simultaneously, and tacks the **2-byte checksum (`0x49D6`)** to the very end of the transmission.  

*   **`crc16_rx.vhd` (The Receiver):** Swallows the whole 7-byte frame. Because modulo-2 math is magic, a clean frame run through the engine will always leave a **final residue of `0x0000`**. If the residue is anything else, the data is trashed and the system raises an **error flag**.  

*   **`crc16_top.vhd` (Channel & Fault Injector):** Hooks the TX and RX together. Since we can't easily simulate random electrical noise, this block uses an **XOR gate to flip specific bits mid-transit** so you can actively test and verify the error detection mechanisms.
